From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: xhci: fail system suspend while a USB2 port is resuming

A device can signal remote wakeup right after usbcore suspended its
port. xhci_bus_suspend() refuses the bus suspend for a resuming port
only when root hub wakeup is enabled, which it isn't by default, and
xhci-plat's suspend doesn't look at HCD_FLAG_WAKEUP_PENDING. For a USB2
port only GetPortStatus from the hub driver or a root hub resume writes
U0, and neither runs during system suspend, so the port stays in Resume.

xhci_suspend() then halts the controller and the system goes to sleep
with the port in Resume. On SC8280XP with both PCIe links in D3 during
that sleep, the port no longer answers after resume, the device is
disconnected, re-enumeration hangs and the xHCI dies; the keyboard cover
behind it is gone until the next reboot.

Before halting the controller, look for a connected USB2 port that was
suspended by usbcore or by bus suspend and is now in Resume, and fail
the suspend. The PM core resumes the root hubs, and the resume of the
port is completed while the system resumes. A port whose resume timed
out is no longer marked suspended, so it can't block suspend for good.
Don't depend on do_wakeup: on dwc3 it follows the probe order, and the
port state is what matters.

Signed-off-by: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 drivers/usb/host/xhci.c | 41 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 41 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/host/xhci.c b/drivers/usb/host/xhci.c
index 92b50d01c..91bd5fae8 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/host/xhci.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/xhci.c
@@ -942,6 +942,37 @@ static bool xhci_pending_portevent(struct xhci_hcd *xhci)
 	return false;
 }
 
+/*
+ * A device can signal remote wakeup after usbcore suspended its USB2 port.
+ * Only GetPortStatus from the hub driver or a root hub resume writes U0 for
+ * that, and neither runs during system suspend: the port stays in Resume.
+ * Only look at ports suspended by usbcore or by bus suspend, so that a port
+ * that is stuck in Resume for another reason can't block suspend for good.
+ */
+static bool xhci_port_resuming(struct xhci_hcd *xhci)
+{
+	struct xhci_hub *rhub = &xhci->usb2_rhub;
+	struct xhci_bus_state *bus_state = &rhub->bus_state;
+	u32 portsc;
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < rhub->num_ports; i++) {
+		if (!(bus_state->suspended_ports & BIT(i)) &&
+		    !test_bit(i, &bus_state->bus_suspended))
+			continue;
+
+		portsc = xhci_portsc_readl(rhub->ports[i]);
+		if ((portsc & PORT_CONNECT) &&
+		    (portsc & PORT_PLS_MASK) == XDEV_RESUME) {
+			xhci_info(xhci, "port %d-%d resuming, aborting suspend\n",
+				  rhub->hcd->self.busnum, i + 1);
+			return true;
+		}
+	}
+
+	return false;
+}
+
 /*
  * Stop HC (not bus-specific)
  *
@@ -977,6 +1008,16 @@ int xhci_suspend(struct xhci_hcd *xhci, bool do_wakeup)
 	    (xhci->shared_hcd && xhci->shared_hcd->state != HC_STATE_SUSPENDED))
 		return -EINVAL;
 
+	/*
+	 * Halting the controller now would leave such a port in Resume across
+	 * system sleep, and not every host gets it back afterwards. Fail the
+	 * suspend instead; the resume of the port is then completed while the
+	 * system resumes. This doesn't depend on do_wakeup: the device was armed
+	 * by usbcore, and the port state is what matters.
+	 */
+	if (HCD_HW_ACCESSIBLE(hcd) && xhci_port_resuming(xhci))
+		return -EBUSY;
+
 	/* Clear root port wake on bits if wakeup not allowed. */
 	xhci_disable_hub_port_wake(xhci, &xhci->usb3_rhub, do_wakeup);
 	xhci_disable_hub_port_wake(xhci, &xhci->usb2_rhub, do_wakeup);
